WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.253
This is a wasp next.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.560
In the wild, some of these nests are built

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.900
underground using clay or mud.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.271
But you might be most familiar
with the paper nests --

00:00:10.271 --> 00:00:12.238
the ones built out of saliva and pulp,

00:00:12.238 --> 00:00:15.030
typically found attached
to buildings or trees.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.610
And these guys can get big.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.660
According to the Guinness
Book of World Records,

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.890
the largest wasp nest was 12 feet long

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:23.613
and 18 feet all around.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.480
Now, we're all accustomed
to avoiding these nests,

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.010
because, obviously, wasps are scary,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.760
but today we're going to go inside one,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.720
because there are a lot
of fascinating factors

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.970
that make these nests work
for wasps and their babies.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:37.769
So, let's cut into one.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.950
Pretty easy to cut through this.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.280
The hardest part was
definitely here at the top,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.780
where you can tell the cells
are more dense and wider.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.140
If I can give you a scale
for how big this is,

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.200
I mean, like, look at it next to my head.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.440
This is like a brick
cellphone from the '90s.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.000
The key to this type of nest

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.440
is the pedicel, or the root.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.860
It's the foundation that the
entire nest is built off of.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.770
These are the cells.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.280
Wasps are like butterflies.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.940
They're what's called
holometabolous insects,

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.610
meaning they go through a metamorphosis

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.295
before they become adults,

00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:20.193
and these cells are basically their cribs.

00:01:20.193 --> 00:01:21.575
Petrovicheva: The cells are open when they

00:01:21.575 --> 00:01:23.792
first are produced.

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:26.100
The queen will lay their egg in there,

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.620
and then it'll get sealed off.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.163
They're shaped similar
to a hollow cylinder,

00:01:30.163 --> 00:01:32.285
or like a regular lead school pencil,

00:01:32.285 --> 00:01:33.663
and there's a reason why.

00:01:33.663 --> 00:01:35.940
Petrovicheva: The walls of the hexagon

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.380
hold the other cells.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.040
Also, they're a compact space,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.300
so you can get a lot of these cells

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.440
in very, very small space

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.180
while still having a
very strong structure.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.597
Mills: They're also perfectly sized

00:01:48.597 --> 00:01:51.505
to fit both the larva and its roommates.

00:01:51.505 --> 00:01:53.145
Petrovicheva: Oftentimes there's some food

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:55.810
laid in with the egg, so when it hatches

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.280
it has the food in there already.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.070
The drones move from cell to cell

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.682
in a circular motion,

00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:02.750
making sure that each larva has food.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.700
Some wasp species tear up
insects for the babies,

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.110
but other wasps take things further.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.690
Mason wasps drop off whole caterpillars

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.460
for their children to eat alive,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.900
while tarantula hawk wasps lay their eggs

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.150
on tarantulas they sting and paralyze

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.890
as a birthday feast.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.560
Other species lay their
eggs inside their prey,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.060
and once the larvae hatch,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.251
they have a meal waiting for them

00:02:24.251 --> 00:02:25.990
to chew their way out of.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:29.394
Petrovicheva: Once the larva
is ready to metamorphose,

00:02:29.394 --> 00:02:33.200
it'll become a pupa inside the cell still,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.490
and then it'll hatch as an adult.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.470
Mills: Since the cells are both the crib

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.650
and the cocoon for these wasp babies,

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.190
the nest must maintain
a stable temperature

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.850
with high humidity.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.480
This means as much insulation as possible.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.353
For these types of nests,

00:02:50.353 --> 00:02:54.090
the domes are almost always
made of salivary secretions,

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.790
plant material, or paper or cardboard.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:01.640
Petrovicheva: So, they
take wood, they chew it up,

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.690
and they mix it with
saliva to make a glue,

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.920
and then they lay it in thin layers.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.524
The denser the layers,

00:03:09.524 --> 00:03:13.130
the stronger and more
sturdy the structure.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.500
After building the core structure,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.070
the queen wraps the entire
nest in an envelope --

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.820
these thin sheets of macerated pulp.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.020
This layer basically protects the comb,

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.680
or each layer of cells,

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.400
limiting the entrance
to one tiny little hole.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.635
Scientists think this helps

00:03:28.635 --> 00:03:30.393
to maintain the internal temperature

00:03:30.393 --> 00:03:31.910
and humidity of the structure.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.890
And more cones can be added
on with more pedicels,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.450
sort of like expanding a mansion

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.100
with tiny little staircases
that the wasps can use

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.450
to get from one area to another.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.602
So, now I know the question
on some of your minds is,

00:03:44.602 --> 00:03:46.850
"Is there honey in a wasp nest?"

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.070
And the answer is no.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:51.090
While both bees and
wasps pollinate flowers,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.870
bees actually farm nectar
to turn it into honey,

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:55.490
the food source for their larvae.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.570
Most wasps, on the other
hand, are meat eaters

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.280
and prefer a diet of
freshly chewed insects,

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.490
meaning you won't find
honey in their nests,

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.600
but you might find a lot of dead bugs.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.460
So, the next time you
see one of these nests

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.318
and you're tempted to knock it down,

00:04:09.318 --> 00:04:10.910
maybe back off.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.670
Those wasps put a lot of effort

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:14.810
into building this crib for their babies.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.535
We don't want to tick them off.

